<!--
  Apex Weather mark: the sun setting behind the Apex Maps chevron.

  The ridge is the lower chevron of the Apex Maps A-sharp glyph, unchanged, at the scale and
  translate that glyph has always had. The upper chevron is gone; the sun stands where its peak was,
  because the icon opens a weather app and an arrow says nothing about weather. What is left of the
  family resemblance is the ridge and the palette - amber and cool blue on night sky.

  The sun is centred on the peak and drawn *behind* it: its lower edge is the disc with the ridge's
  own silhouette, pushed 8 units outward, cut out of it. That offset is the gap, and the gap is what
  makes the peak read as standing in front of the sun rather than resting on it. The cut is taken
  against the two rays running down from the apex, not against the infinite lines through them - the
  lines would carry on upward past the apex and slice the disc in half.

  The rays are not decoration. Without them the disc and the chevron read as head and shoulders once
  Android flattens them to one colour for a themed icon. See ic_launcher_monochrome.xml. Only the
  five upper rays are drawn; a ray pointing down would run into the ridge.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp" android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108" android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group android:scaleX="0.27" android:scaleY="0.27"
